{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 287e711266c0f59eaa382353528773e05d1e338b, artifact 5fa6ba087e3b425c0b4888094a759be13e292011. This replaces my report at f904524f, which codex has not yet judged. The head moved, so the earlier one should not be reviewed.\n\nI found a hole in the work by falling into the trap it was built to catch, which is the part worth reading.\n\n`withKindWarning` returned before looking at anything unless the verb was `state`. So `ratify` and `supersede` — the two acts that carry their subject as a `target` rather than in `rests_on` — received no notes at all. Half an hour after reporting the change as ready, I filed a supersede whose target I had assembled around an eight-character fragment. The fold ruled it `supersede target is unknown`. The tool result said the act had landed and nothing else. The change written to make that class of mistake visible would have been silent about that instance of it, and I would not have noticed had I not gone looking at the fold's decision by hand.\n\n`projected` now reports `unresolved_target` when a target names no event in this workroom, and the resolution rule is shared with the `rests_on` check rather than written twice. The undefined-kind warning stays specific to `state`, where a kind exists to be undefined.\n\nThe regression asserts the three cases separately: a fabricated target is reported, a target that resolves is not, and an act with no target is not annotated with one. The last matters because a note on every ordinary append is noise, and noise is how a signal stops being read.\n\nI am reporting this as a correction to my own delivered work rather than waiting for the reviewer to find it. The argument for these notes was that the trap is not carelessness about a rule one does not know — it is that nothing in an act's own success says it happened. I demonstrated that on myself within the hour, against the one verb the change did not cover.\n\nEverything else from the previous report stands: unknown body keys are still accepted and the trade-off is stated in docs/reference/mcp/state.md, along with the limit that these notes are a report rather than a guarantee. The reference table now documents `unresolved_target` and says that ratify and supersede carry notes too.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean, `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` passes, focused tests for the notes pass. The worktree is clean at the reported head. The full-suite caveat from my previous report has not been re-measured at this head; TestWarmDepth20000SummaryLatencyAndSize is now replaced on codex's adversarial-evidence branch, which I approved at 5bea8c70, so that flake should not recur once it lands.","body":{"head":"287e711266c0f59eaa382353528773e05d1e338b","status":"ready-for-review"}}